    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost). If you happen to be working with leftover holiday ham instead of buying it fresh, this recipe gets dramatically cheaper, because the ham is the single biggest line on the list.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eLeftover ham is free ham.\u003c/strong\u003e This recipe exists to use up the end of a holiday roast. If you are buying, watch for whole hams on markdown after a holiday and freeze the diced meat in bags.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned white beans skip the overnight soak.\u003c/strong\u003e Dried beans cost less if you plan ahead, but canned means you can decide to make this at four in the afternoon.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eChicken broth is the second biggest line, so buy the big cartons.\u003c/strong\u003e Store brand is fine here. Between the ham and the tomatoes, this soup is already well seasoned.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCarrots, celery and onion are the cheap backbone.\u003c/strong\u003e Buy them whole rather than pre chopped and the entire vegetable base costs less than the ham trimmings."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Dice the onion, carrots and celery, and drain and rinse the white beans.",
                    "Heat a large pot and cook the diced ham until the edges brown and it renders a little fat. Browning the ham first is what gives this soup its backbone.",
                    "Add the onion, carrots and celery to the pot and cook until they soften and pick up the browned bits.",
                    "Stir in the chili powder, dried parsley and black pepper and cook for a minute.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth and the diced tomatoes and bring everything up to a simmer.",
                    "Add the drained white beans and simmer gently, mashing a scoop of the beans against the side of the pot to thicken the broth naturally.",
                    "Taste for salt, and cook the rice separately to serve it under the soup."
                ],
